usage_frame: >
  YAWN is an orientation shape for inference before action. Use it by naming
  the field, orienting the observer, recording what is being inferred, naming
  the missing bridge, choosing one bounded move, protecting agency, gathering
  proof, and updating memory through replay. Any AI can help mirror and
  organize, but the human remains the source of authorization.

stable_boundary: >
  .yawn does not claim reality is literally code or that a stored arena is the
  world. It works with the practical fact that humans and software agents
  receive partial signals, make inferences, act from models, and update memory
  through attributed feedback.

question_packet_rule: >
  Questions are projections over the ontology, not the ontology. A question
  packet maps plain-language answers into attributed semantic records and turns
  skipped answers into visible lacunae rather than invented content.

orientation_map_rule: >
  Nine stable semantic axes hold attributed, revisable orientation coverage.
  The versioned inquiry policy deterministically proposes one useful question,
  up to three visible paths, or an explicit hold, then emits a closed replay
  receipt. Wording, order, medium, pacing, density, and accessibility remain
  View concerns; they do not change the semantic hash, truth, or authority.

objective_compiler_rule: >
  Dialogue is a programming surface. Preserve the source; expose typed
  detections and structural consequences; keep objective candidate,
  ratification, objective Yawn, sleeping bot, activation, and effect authority
  as separate records. The root .yawn routes objective holons as an alignment
  bridge rather than owning every objective.

folder_node_rule: >
  Every active folder is a knowledge node and mental-model node. Each active
  folder should contain node.yawn as its local orientation card.

